WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.439
A merchant earns a badge for selling on many days in a row.

2
00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.783
Here are its first two weeks of June.

3
00:00:08.783 --> 00:00:11.381
White days had sales, grey days had none.

4
00:00:11.567 --> 00:00:16.127
A run of days with no gap between them is a streak, also called an island.

5
00:00:16.127 --> 00:00:18.961
Where does each one start, and how long is it?

6
00:00:19.167 --> 00:00:22.593
GROUP BY needs a value that every day of a streak shares.

7
00:00:22.593 --> 00:00:29.207
Grouping by the day gives ten groups of one, and a query has no counter that carries from one row to the next.

8
00:00:29.367 --> 00:00:30.504
Here's the trick.

9
00:00:30.504 --> 00:00:35.524
Number the sales days in order with row number: one, two, three, and so on.

10
00:00:35.967 --> 00:00:39.616
Now subtract each row's number from its date.

11
00:00:39.616 --> 00:00:41.643
June 3 minus 1 is June 2.

12
00:00:41.643 --> 00:00:44.157
June 4 minus 2 is June 2 again.

13
00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:49.875
Inside a streak, the date and the number both go up by one, so the difference stays the same.

14
00:00:50.067 --> 00:00:51.812
Then comes a missing day.

15
00:00:51.812 --> 00:00:57.258
The date jumps by two, but the number only by one, so the difference moves up.

16
00:00:57.258 --> 00:00:59.004
June 7 minus 4 is June 3.

17
00:00:59.133 --> 00:01:03.215
Every gap bumps it again, so every streak gets a value of its own.

18
00:01:03.433 --> 00:01:05.316
That value is an island id.

19
00:01:05.316 --> 00:01:12.705
Group by it, and each streak folds into one row: its first day, its last day, and how many days it lasted.

20
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:17.078
The longest streak is four days, from the 11th to the 14th of June.

21
00:01:17.078 --> 00:01:19.697
The gaps never had to be found one by one.

22
00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:25.557
On the course's large data set, merchant 1000 sold on 160 of 300 days.

23
00:01:25.557 --> 00:01:32.265
The same query finds its longest streak: six days, from the 6th to the 11th of May.

24
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:37.450
In a streak, the date and the row number climb together, so their difference stays put.

25
00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.715
A gap makes it jump, and the difference names the island.
